<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Where Did the Days of the Week Come From?</title>

    <style>
        /* Universal Selector */
        * {
            box-sizing: border-box;
            font-family: Arial;
        }

        /* Element Selector */
        p {
            font-size: 16px;
            line-height: 1.5;
        }

        /* ID Selector */
        #important {
            background-color: lightblue;
            padding: 10px;
        }
    </style>
</head>

<body>

    <center>
        <h1>Where Did the Days of the Week Come From?</h1>
        <p>A short look at the history behind the names of our days.</p>
    </center>

    <hr>

    <h2>How Did They Get Their Names?</h2>

    <p>
        The names of the seven days come from a mix of ancient Roman
        and Germanic traditions. People connected different days with
        the Sun, Moon, planets, and gods.
    </p>

    <div id="important">
        <h2>The Days of the Week</h2>

        <p>
            <b>Sunday</b> came from the Sun, while <b>Monday</b> came from
            the Moon. <b>Tuesday</b> was connected to the Norse god Tyr,
            and <b>Wednesday</b> was connected to Odin or Woden.
        </p>

        <p>
            <b>Thursday</b> was named after Thor, and <b>Friday</b> was
            connected to the goddess Frigg. <b>Saturday</b> came from
            Saturn, a Roman god.
        </p>
    </div>

    <hr>

    <h2>Was There One Person Who Invented Them?</h2>

    <p>
        No. The names were not invented by one person. They developed
        over a very long time as different cultures influenced each other.
        The names we use today are the result of those old traditions.
    </p>

    <hr>

    <center>
        <p><b>Fun Fact:</b> The seven-day week has been used for thousands of years!</p>
    </center>

</body>
</html>
